      "plain_alpha_wave_connection": "Alpha is not a direct measure of calmness, intelligence, or learning ability.",
      "plain_alpha_wave_example": "Opening the eyes can change the prominence of this rhythm.",
      "plain_alpha_wave_explanation": "Brain rhythms change with what a person is doing. They are not permanent labels for people.",
      "plain_alpha_wave_idea": "Alpha is often noticeable over the back of the head when someone rests with eyes closed.",
      "plain_alpha_wave_question": "A person opens their eyes and the recording changes. Which explanation fits?",
      "plain_awake_connection": "A single brainwave frequency cannot tell exactly what someone is thinking.",
      "plain_awake_example": "Listening quietly and solving a puzzle can both happen while awake.",
      "plain_awake_explanation": "Wakefulness covers many activities. Brain recordings depend on the task, the person, and recording conditions.",
      "plain_awake_idea": "Wakefulness includes many changing patterns of brain activity.",
      "plain_awake_question": "Two awake people are doing different tasks. What should we expect?",
      "plain_axon_hillock_connection": "The usual starting region is the axon initial segment, next to the hillock; they are not identical.",
      "plain_axon_hillock_example": "Incoming signals can bring a cell close enough to its firing threshold for a spike to begin.",
      "plain_axon_hillock_explanation": "Many neurons start spikes in the initial segment beside the hillock. The required threshold varies between cells and conditions.",
      "plain_axon_hillock_idea": "The hillock joins the cell body to the axon; a nearby region often starts an electrical spike.",
      "plain_axon_hillock_question": "Incoming activity reaches the level needed to start a spike near the start of an axon. What happens?",
      "plain_axon_terminal_connection": "The next cell's receptors help determine its response; a chemical messenger does not guarantee a new spike.",
      "plain_axon_terminal_example": "A message reaching an axon's end can trigger release of neurotransmitters.",
      "plain_axon_terminal_explanation": "At chemical synapses, release and receptor responses help pass information between cells. The response depends on the receiving cell.",
      "plain_axon_terminal_idea": "At many connections, the end of an axon releases chemical messengers toward the next cell.",
      "plain_axon_terminal_question": "An electrical signal reaches an axon's end. What can happen at a chemical synapse?",
      "plain_beta_wave_connection": "Muscle activity and recording conditions can also affect faster activity.",
      "plain_beta_wave_example": "A recording during a task may contain beta alongside other rhythms.",
      "plain_beta_wave_explanation": "Different rhythms can occur together. Interpretation needs the task and recording context.",
      "plain_beta_wave_idea": "Beta names a faster range of brain-recording rhythms seen in many waking contexts.",
      "plain_beta_wave_question": "A recording contains faster activity during a task. What is a careful interpretation?",
      "plain_delta_wave_connection": "Meaning depends on age, location, state, and recording conditions.",
      "plain_delta_wave_example": "Slow rhythms can be prominent during deep non-REM sleep.",
      "plain_delta_wave_explanation": "A frequency range describes a pattern. Context is needed to interpret what that pattern may mean.",
      "plain_delta_wave_idea": "Delta names a slow range of rhythms in electrical brain recordings.",
      "plain_delta_wave_question": "A recording includes slow rhythms. What should be checked before interpreting them?",
      "plain_dendrite_connection": "Incoming signals can influence whether a cell sends a signal onward.",
      "plain_dendrite_example": "While you listen, nerve cells receive input from other cells.",
      "plain_dendrite_explanation": "Dendrites receive input; how the cell responds depends on the combined activity of its inputs and its own properties.",
      "plain_dendrite_idea": "Receives many of the signals arriving from other nerve cells.",
      "plain_dendrite_question": "A cell receives several incoming messages. Which contribution is highlighted?",
      "plain_eeg_note": "Frequency bands name recording patterns, not types of people. An EEG cannot read thoughts or measure intelligence.",
      "plain_gamma_wave_connection": "Fast muscle activity can affect recordings, and gamma is not a simple consciousness meter.",
      "plain_gamma_wave_example": "Researchers can study fast activity during different tasks.",
      "plain_gamma_wave_explanation": "Fast activity requires careful interpretation. A single rhythm cannot settle what a person is experiencing.",
      "plain_gamma_wave_idea": "Gamma names a high-frequency range studied in brain activity.",
      "plain_gamma_wave_question": "A researcher observes high-frequency activity. What must they consider?",
      "plain_lgn_visual_connection": "It is one stop in a connected pathway, not the place where all seeing happens.",
      "plain_lgn_visual_example": "Signals about a scene pass through this relay before reaching primary visual cortex.",
      "plain_lgn_visual_explanation": "The relay helps process and pass information onward. Seeing depends on the wider system.",
      "plain_lgn_visual_idea": "A relay in the thalamus that passes much visual information toward visual cortex.",
      "plain_lgn_visual_question": "Visual information passes through a thalamic relay. Which description fits?",
      "plain_myelin_connection": "Support cells make myelin; gaps between the layers also help signals travel.",
