      "what_microbial_forensics_is": "What microbial forensics is",
      "what_s_happening": "What's happening",
      "what_shapes_it": "What shapes it",
      "what_shapes_it_2": "What shapes it",
      "what_surveillance_is": "What surveillance is",
      "what_synthetic_biology_is": "What synthetic biology is",
      "what_the_gut_brain_axis_is": "What the gut-brain axis is",
      "what_the_virome_is": "What the virome is",
      "what_therapeutic_mabs_are": "What therapeutic mAbs are",
      "what_this_framing_cannot_do": "What this framing cannot do",
      "what_this_means_for_everyday_life": "What this means for everyday life",
      "what_this_means_for_families_schools": "What this means for families + schools",
      "what_we_should_not_overstate": "What we should not overstate: ",
      "what_we_should_not_overstate_2": "What we should not overstate: ",
      "what_we_should_not_overstate_3": "What we should not overstate: ",
      "what_we_should_not_overstate_4": "What we should not overstate: ",
      "what_you_can_do_at_any_life_stage": "What you can do, at any life stage: ",
      "when_a_hospital_sees_unexpected_cluste": "When a hospital sees unexpected clusters of MRSA, C. difficile, Candida auris, or other healthcare-associated infections, the infection-prevention team uses microbial forensics to trace transmission. Whole-genome sequencing distinguishes between (a) coincidental cases (different strains), (b) common-source transmission (all from one contaminated piece of equipment, water source, or asymptomatic carrier), (c) person-to-person spread via healthcare workers. The Mid-Atlantic Region NHSN Healthcare Reporting database connects to genomic surveillance via the CDC. The investigation guides specific interventions: equipment replacement, deep cleaning, staff testing, patient cohorting.",
      "when_a_pathogen_enters_only_the_lympho": "When a pathogen enters, only the lymphocytes whose receptors happen to fit react. They get activated. The other 99.99...% sit out the infection.",
      "when_a_pathogen_kills_people_microbial": "When a pathogen kills people, microbial forensics asks: where did it come from? The discipline traces foodborne-outbreak sources, hospital-acquired infections, environmental contamination, and (rarely + consequentially) deliberate attacks. The 2001 anthrax letters launched the field; foodborne-outbreak investigations + COVID origins debates have shaped its modern practice. The science is real, the political stakes are sometimes enormous, and the limits of attribution from genome data are real + worth understanding.",
      "when_a_phage_injects_dna_cas_proteins_": "When a phage injects DNA, Cas proteins clip a small piece of the invader's DNA and integrate it into the bacterium's own genome at a special locus called the CRISPR array. Each piece is a \"spacer.\" The bacterium now carries a record of the attack.",
      "when_bacteria_evolve_resistance_to_a_p": "When bacteria evolve resistance to a phage, scientists can re-isolate phages from sewage or soil that have already evolved counter-resistance. The arms race never stops.",
      "when_it_finds_a_pam_it_tries_to_base_p": "When it finds a PAM, it tries to base-pair the guide RNA with the adjacent DNA. If 20 bases match (or close to it), Cas9 commits.",
      "when_memory_fails": "When memory fails: ",
      "when_people_picture_an_emerging_infect": "When people picture an emerging infectious disease, they picture a virus. But the past 20 years have seen fungi rise into a serious + underappreciated category of human + ecological pathogen. Climate change, agricultural fungicide overuse, expanding immunocompromised populations, and very thin antifungal pipelines have combined to create a problem we are only beginning to take seriously.",
      "when_people_picture_climate_change_the": "When people picture climate change, they picture smokestacks + cars + coal plants. The microbial layer is invisible + far larger. Microorganisms produce + consume most of Earth's greenhouse gases; soil + ocean microbes set the carbon-cycle balance; permafrost microbes hold a billion-year-old carbon stockpile that is now waking up. Climate science + microbial ecology are inseparable.",
      "when_the_virome_goes_wrong": "When the virome goes wrong",
      "when_to_use_it": "When to use it",
      "when_we_talk_about_the_microbiome_most": "When we talk about \"the microbiome,\" most coverage is about bacteria. But for every bacterial cell in or on the human body, there are roughly 10× more virus particles. The human virome is the total collection of viruses living in us: bacteriophages (viruses that infect our gut + skin + oral bacteria, vastly outnumbering everything else), eukaryotic viruses (those that infect our human cells, including persistent latent ones), and endogenous retroviruses (viral DNA permanently integrated into our genome from infections in our distant ancestors). Most virome residents are not making us sick. Many appear to be neutral or even beneficial. We are walking, eating, sleeping ecosystems.",
      "where": "Where",
      "where_2": "Where",
      "where_it_happens": "Where it happens",
      "where_it_lives": "Where it lives",
      "where_it_s_going": "Where it's going: ",
      "where_it_stands_clinically": "Where it stands clinically: ",
      "white_blood_cells": "White blood cells",
      "who_declares_smallpox_eradicated_the_o": "WHO declares smallpox eradicated - the only human disease eliminated.",
      "who_global_outbreak_alert_response_net": "WHO Global Outbreak Alert + Response Network (GOARN); CDC Global Disease Detection Centers; the Wildlife Conservation Society's One Health monitoring. Most pandemics are caught in the first weeks → can be contained if local response is fast.",
      "whole_genome_sequencing": "Whole-genome sequencing",
      "whose_virome_are_you": "Whose virome are you?",
      "why_biofilms_make_antibiotic_resistanc": "Why biofilms make antibiotic resistance worse: ",
      "why_fungal_infections_are_rising": "Why fungal infections are rising",
      "why_hgt_shapes_the_resistance_crisis": "Why HGT shapes the resistance crisis: ",
      "why_it_matters": "Why it matters",
      "why_it_matters_2": "Why it matters",
      "why_organ_transplants_are_rejected": "Why organ transplants are rejected: ",
      "why_phages_are_uniquely_promising": "Why phages are uniquely promising:",
      "why_so_many_in_the_first_2_years": "Why so many in the first 2 years? ",
      "why_the_pipeline_is_empty": "Why the pipeline is empty",
      "why_this_is_remarkable": "Why this is remarkable: ",
      "why_this_matters_clinically": "Why this matters clinically: ",
      "why_vaccines_work": "Why vaccines work: ",
      "why_women_are_more_susceptible": "Why women are more susceptible: ",
      "you_do_not_see_public_health_surveilla": "You do not see public health surveillance until it fails. The systems that scan wastewater for polio, sequence E. coli outbreaks to find their source, trace contacts during an Ebola scare, and track excess mortality in real time during a pandemic are how a society defends itself against infectious disease. They are also chronically underfunded, institutionally fragmented, and politically vulnerable. School-based health workers + counselors are often the closest most students get to the system.",
      "your_answer": "Your answer: ",
