      "replace_target": "Replace target",
      "replace_target_uses_nata_s_125_recomme": "Replace target uses NATA's 125% recommendation (midpoint of the 100-150% guideline) to account for ongoing post-exercise sweat and urine loss.",
      "report_content_that_promotes_eating_di": "Report content that promotes eating disorders. ",
      "research_shows_kids_who_eat_breakfast_": "Research shows kids who eat breakfast at school perform better academically, have fewer behavioral incidents, and have better attendance. Removing the application step means kids who qualify but were embarrassed to apply now eat. Maine's law also expanded local-food sourcing into school cafeterias.",
      "resources_including_helplines_that_can": "Resources, including helplines that can help right now",
      "resting_energy_distribution_by_organ": "Resting energy distribution by organ",
      "rigid_rituals_around_food_exercise_or_": "Rigid rituals around food, exercise, or daily structure that disrupt normal life",
      "roasted_chicken_sweet_potato_peas": "Roasted chicken, sweet potato, peas",
      "roughly_0_85_g_kg_body_weight_varies_w": "Roughly 0.85 g/kg body weight; varies widely with body size.",
      "roughly_60_of_your_body_weight_is_wate": "Roughly 60% of your body weight is water. Every cell uses it. The brain runs about 75% water and the first thing that drops with mild dehydration (1-2% body-weight fluid loss) is attention, short-term memory, and mood. This lab covers four things: how much fluid you actually need, how to read your own body for status, which drinks help and which do not, and how to handle hydration as a student athlete. Hydration is never a substitute for eating. If you find yourself using water to feel full or skip meals, talk to a trusted adult and call NEDA at 1-800-931-2237.",
      "rural_distance_seasonal_employment_rea": "Rural distance + seasonal employment = real food-access issues",
