      "stoop_energy_example_10k": "Illustrative calculation: a 1 kg object at about 141 m/s carries about 10,000 J.",
      "stoop_energy_example_5k": "Illustrative calculation: a 1 kg object at 100 m/s carries 5,000 J.",
      "stoop_energy_reference_note": "Reference energies for scale, not measured species records or energy delivered in a collision.",
      "stoop_fact_0_text": "From rest, speed approaches terminal gradually. The time to reach a chosen fraction depends on mass, drag coefficient and frontal area. More starting height can allow more acceleration, but a finite fall need not get close to terminal.",
      "stoop_fact_0_title": "A limit, not a finish line",
      "stoop_fact_1_text": "A real falcon changes wing posture throughout the dive. Here Cd = 0.18 is an adjustable illustrative input held constant. It is not a universal measured coefficient for every bird, angle or speed.",
      "stoop_fact_1_title": "Posture changes drag",
      "stoop_fact_2_text": "Holding everything else fixed, greater mass raises this model’s terminal speed, while greater frontal area lowers it. Real birds change several of these properties together; comparing one slider does not rank species.",
      "stoop_fact_2_title": "Separate mass from frontal area",
      "stoop_fact_3_text": "A falcon redirects its flight before or after an interception. Lift, turning radius and changing posture matter during that maneuver. This vertical-fall model ends at the ground or the time limit and does not simulate a pull-up.",
      "stoop_fact_3_title": "A real pull-up changes the problem",
      "stoop_fact_4_text": "Kinetic energy is one half of mass times speed squared. The upper energy display uses terminal speed; the scrubber shows the speed actually sampled during this modeled fall. Neither directly measures energy transferred to prey.",
      "stoop_fact_4_title": "Energy depends on the sampled speed",
      "stoop_fact_5_text": "The graph samples every 0.05 seconds for up to 12 seconds, stopping sooner if it reaches the ground. A graph that ends aloft is a partial flight, not a collision or a completed dive.",
      "stoop_fact_5_title": "Read the observation window",
      "stoop_facts_header": "The stoop: height becomes speed",
      "stoop_facts_overview": "A peregrine changes its wing and body posture during a dive, changing the aerodynamic forces it experiences. This calculator isolates one idea: how gravity and quadratic drag shape a vertical fall. Its fixed posture and still-air assumptions are teaching simplifications, not a reconstruction of a particular bird or a speed record.",
