      "roadready_auto_detects_a_connected_gam": "ការគ្រប់គ្រងដៃហ្គេមលំនាំដើម៖ ដំបងឆ្វេងបង្វិលចង្កូត ដំបងស្តាំងាកមើលចំហៀង RT បង្កើនល្បឿន LT ជាន់ហ្វ្រាំង A ស៊ីផ្លេ B ប្តូរកាមេរ៉ា គ្រាប់ចុចទិសឡើង/ចុះជ្រើសទៅមុខ/ថយក្រោយ ឆ្វេង/ស្តាំបើកភ្លើងស៊ីញ៉ូ ថយក្រោយ/ទិដ្ឋភាពជ្រើសចត និងចាប់ផ្តើមផ្អាក។ ប្រើការគ្រប់គ្រងដើម្បីមើល ឬប្តូរការកំណត់ប៊ូតុង។",
      "roadready_driving_hud_shows_current_sp": "RoadReady driving HUD: shows current speed in mph, score, elapsed time, fuel, and active warnings (seatbelt, speed limit, hazards). Controls: W or up arrow to accelerate, S or down arrow to brake, A and D or left and right arrows to steer, C to cycle camera, L to toggle high beams, Space to pause, Escape to end the drive.",
      "roadready_error": "🚗 RoadReady Error",
      "roadready_practice_driving_log": "RoadReady — Practice Driving Log",
      "roadready_study_cheat_sheet": "RoadReady Study Cheat Sheet",
      "roll_through_they_re_probably_right": "Roll through — they're probably right",
      "rolling_content": "Tires are flexible. They squish at the contact patch, unsquish at the back — and some energy is lost to heat every rotation. That loss is called ROLLING RESISTANCE. Unlike drag, it barely changes with speed. It depends on: tire inflation (underinflated = wasted fuel, about 0.2% MPG per 1 PSI low, up to about 3%), tire compound (low-rolling-resistance LRR tires save ~4-8% fuel), road surface (asphalt < concrete < gravel), and vehicle weight. Below about 40 mph, rolling resistance is the DOMINANT force slowing you down. Above 40 mph, aerodynamic drag takes over.",
      "rollover_suv_on_curve": "Rollover (SUV on curve)",
      "roundabout_desc": "Yield to traffic already in the circle. Enter on a gap. Keep moving unless safety requires a stop. Common in Maine new roads.",
      "roundabout_drill": "Roundabout Drill",
      "roundabout_drill_2": "Roundabout Drill",
      "roundabout_drill_yield_on_entry_signal": "Roundabout drill. Yield on entry, signal on exit.",
