      "arc_m_steady_title": "Steady hand",
      "arc_m_steady_why": "Faster travel puts less metal and heat into each inch, so that stretch of bead comes out narrower.",
      "arc_m_thinal_ask": "From the starting settings, travelling 4 in/min faster will make penetration:",
      "arc_m_thinal_goal": "Weld 1/8\" aluminum with no burn-through and at least 60% penetration.",
      "arc_m_thinal_h1": "Aluminum pulls heat away fast, but 1/8\" plate has very little metal to absorb it. The window is narrow.",
      "arc_m_thinal_h2": "Keep amperage and nudge travel speed up 1 in/min at a time; watch the root in the macro-etch.",
      "arc_m_thinal_status": "Penetration {value1} of the plate (need at least 60%). Burn-through severity: {value2}.",
      "arc_m_thinal_title": "Thin aluminum, no holes",
      "arc_m_thinal_why": "Heat input is V × A × 60 ÷ travel speed, so moving faster puts less heat into each inch, and penetration follows heat input.",
      "arc_m_undercut_ask": "Slowing travel from 22 to 14 in/min will leave:",
      "arc_m_undercut_goal": "Start from a hot, fast MIG pass on 3/8\" steel that undercuts. Remove the undercut while keeping penetration near target.",
      "arc_m_undercut_h1": "In this model undercut needs BOTH high amperage and fast travel. Break either condition.",
      "arc_m_undercut_h2": "Slowing down adds heat, so check the macro-etch for burn-through as you go.",
      "arc_m_undercut_status": "Undercut severity: {value1}. Penetration {value2} of the plate. Verdict: {value3}.",
      "arc_m_undercut_title": "Stop the undercut",
      "arc_m_undercut_why": "High current carves the toes, and fast travel leaves no time to fill them. Slowing down lets filler flow into the groove.",
      "arc_mat_aluminum": "Aluminum 6061 — high thermal conductivity (roughly 3–4× mild steel, about 167 vs 50 W/m·K) means heat spreads fast, so you need more amperage to compensate. Its oxide layer must be removed (AC TIG's cleaning action does it). It shows no temper colours, and it barely glows before it melts — trickier than steel.",
      "arc_mat_stainless": "Stainless 304 — low thermal conductivity (about one-third of mild steel, roughly 16 vs 50 W/m·K) keeps heat concentrated, so warping risk is HIGH. Holding it at roughly 800–1500 °F causes sensitization (chromium carbides), which costs corrosion resistance. Heavy heat tint is usually removed by pickling or passivation. Use low heat input and fast travel.",
      "arc_mat_steel": "Mild steel A36 — the workhorse of structural welding. Moderate thermal conductivity means heat stays where you put it. Forgiving, good for learning. Most common metal at Bath Iron Works.",
