      "proc2_sr_g_reset": "Decision guide reset",
      "proc2_sr_jq_no": "Not quite: this is a {value1}",
      "proc2_sr_jq_ok": "Correct: {value1}",
      "proc2_sr_loaded": "Job loaded into the matcher: {value1}",
      "proc2_sr_tab": "{value1} tab",
      "proc2_sr_top": "Best match is now {value1}",
      "proc2_stick_best": "Structural steel, field repair, outdoor work, rusty or dirty metal",
      "proc2_stick_con1": "Slag to chip and brush off after every pass",
      "proc2_stick_con2": "A rod change every minute or two, and the stub end is wasted",
      "proc2_stick_con3": "Burns through thin sheet",
      "proc2_stick_equip": "The lowest entry cost: a basic stick machine needs no gas and no wire feeder. Engine-drive welders for field work cost more.",
      "proc2_stick_ex": "Steel erection, pipeline root passes, farm and heavy-equipment repair",
      "proc2_stick_full": "Shielded Metal Arc Welding",
      "proc2_stick_mat": "Mild and low-alloy steel, stainless, cast iron (nickel rods), hardfacing",
      "proc2_stick_pro1": "Works outdoors in wind: there is no bottled gas to blow away",
      "proc2_stick_pro2": "Simple kit: a power source, two leads and a box of rods",
      "proc2_stick_pro3": "Cellulose rods dig through rust and mill scale",
      "proc2_stick_pro4": "All-position rods, and an engine-drive welder runs it anywhere",
      "proc2_stick_shield": "The flux coating burns in the arc, making a gas shield and a slag blanket that protects the cooling weld. No gas cylinder.",
      "proc2_stick_tag": "A flux-coated rod makes its own shield: the go-anywhere field process",
      "proc2_table_cap": "Points each choice adds or takes away. ✕ = ruled out. Rows marked * are computed from the ratings: outdoors in wind = 2 × (outdoor rating − 3); visible weld, dirty metal, tight budget and must-carry = rating − 3.",
      "proc2_table_choice": "Your choice",
      "proc2_table_hide": "Hide the scoring table",
      "proc2_table_show": "Show the scoring table",
      "proc2_table_total": "Total",
      "proc2_tig_best": "Thin material, stainless and aluminum, sanitary tube, code-quality root passes",
      "proc2_tig_con1": "Slow: the lowest deposition of the arc processes",
      "proc2_tig_con2": "The hardest to learn: two hands and a foot all working",
      "proc2_tig_con3": "The metal must be spotless, and the gas shield cannot take wind",
      "proc2_tig_equip": "Higher cost: AC output for aluminum, a foot pedal, pure argon; water-cooled torches for production.",
      "proc2_tig_ex": "Stainless food-service equipment, brewery and dairy tube, aerospace parts, bicycle frames",
      "proc2_tig_full": "Gas Tungsten Arc Welding",
      "proc2_tig_mat": "Stainless, aluminum, mild steel, chrome-moly, titanium, copper, magnesium",
      "proc2_tig_pro1": "The cleanest welds: no spatter and no slag",
      "proc2_tig_pro2": "A foot pedal or fingertip control sets the heat while you weld",
      "proc2_tig_pro3": "Welds the widest range of metals, very thin ones included",
      "proc2_tig_pro4": "The standard for root passes on critical pipe",
      "proc2_tig_shield": "Argon (sometimes helium) flows through a ceramic cup around a tungsten electrode that does not melt. The filler rod is fed by hand.",
      "proc2_tig_tag": "A tungsten arc plus a hand-fed rod: the most precise arc process",
      "proc2_tjoint": "T-Joint",
      "proc2_tn_ext": "Pick a welded object at home or school (a railing, a trailer, a bike frame, a car door). Set the Job Matcher to match how it was probably made and check whether the top process agrees with what the weld looks like (ripples, spatter, slag marks, spot dimples). Write a one-paragraph process specification for it.",
      "proc2_tn_m1": "\"TIG is the best process\": TIG is the most precise, but it is slow and the hardest to learn. For most shop jobs MIG or flux-core meets the spec faster. Quality means meeting the spec, not maximising difficulty.",
      "proc2_tn_m2": "\"Flux-core is just MIG without gas\": gas-shielded flux-core (FCAW-G) uses gas too. Only self-shielded wire (FCAW-S) does without a cylinder, and it is the one that tolerates wind.",
      "proc2_tn_m3": "\"Oxy-fuel is obsolete\": oxy-fuel still owns brazing copper lines, heating and bending, cutting, and repairs where there is no electricity.",
      "proc2_tn_m4": "\"Spot welding is cheap MIG\": resistance welding uses no arc, no filler and no gas; the nugget forms inside the joint from resistance heating. It is chosen for sub-second cycle times, and its machines are the most expensive here.",
      "proc2_tn_q1": "Set the Job Matcher to \"Outdoors in wind\". Which processes drop, and what do they have in common? Which rise, and what makes their shield wind-proof?",
      "proc2_tn_q2": "Stick has the lowest equipment cost, yet TIG wins the sanitary-stainless job by a wide margin. When does equipment cost decide a job, and when does it barely matter?",
      "proc2_tn_q3": "Load the car-body line preset and open the scoring table. Which factor decides it for spot welding? What would you have to change about the job for MIG to win?",
      "proc2_tn_q4": "A new welder asks \"which one should I learn first?\" Use the ratings (easy to learn, equipment cost) and the range of jobs each process wins in the matcher to argue for an answer.",
