      "run_pair_clear": "Clear pin",
      "run_pair_conditions": "Arc {value1} A · {value2} V · plate {value3} °F · gap {value4} in. · stickout {value5} in.",
      "run_pair_current": "Selected at {value1} in.",
      "run_pair_empty": "Nothing pinned yet. Compare the same pass before and after the plate thins, a gap opens or the stickout changes.",
      "run_pair_help": "Pin the selected slice, then move the bead inspector or choose an event. The pin stays fixed while the selected moment follows the same recorded pass.",
      "run_pair_marker": "Cyan line: pinned slice",
      "run_pair_note": "Both cross-sections use the same scale and the original recorded values. Several conditions may change together; this comparison does not isolate one cause or recalculate your score.",
      "run_pair_pin": "Pin selected slice",
      "run_pair_reference": "Pinned at {value1} in.",
      "run_pair_replace": "Replace pin with selected slice",
      "run_pair_title": "Compare two moments in this pass",
      "run_pq_ctwd": "Your arm tires and the stickout (contact-tip-to-work distance) grows from 1/2 in. toward 1 in. What does a constant-voltage MIG machine do?",
      "run_pq_ctwd_a": "The current rises, so the bead runs hotter",
      "run_pq_ctwd_b": "Voltage drops to zero and the arc goes out",
      "run_pq_ctwd_c": "The current falls, so penetration sags",
      "run_pq_ctwd_d": "Nothing: the wire feed sets everything",
      "run_pq_ctwd_wa": "That is what a SHORTER stickout does.",
      "run_pq_ctwd_wb": "The arc only goes out at extreme lengths; a change this size mostly shifts the current.",
      "run_pq_ctwd_wd": "The wire feed speed is fixed, but the current it draws depends on the stickout.",
      "run_pq_ctwd_why": "A longer stickout means more wire for the current to pass through, so the wire preheats by resistance and the machine delivers fewer amps. Fewer amps, shallower penetration. Bring the torch back down, or nudge the amps up until you do.",
      "run_pq_gap": "Partway along, the fit-up opens into a gap between the plates. If you change nothing, what happens?",
      "run_pq_gap_a": "Nothing much: filler metal bridges the gap",
      "run_pq_gap_b": "It melts deeper: more burn-through risk",
      "run_pq_gap_c": "The bead goes cold and fails to fuse",
      "run_pq_gap_d": "Spatter jumps because the arc gets longer",
      "run_pq_gap_wa": "Some filler does drop into the gap, but that only sags the crown; the heat still drives deeper.",
      "run_pq_gap_wc": "A gap removes metal from under the arc. There is less to melt, so it runs hotter, not colder.",
      "run_pq_gap_wd": "In this model spatter comes from more amps than the wire runs cleanly, not from a gap.",
      "run_pq_gap_why": "With less metal under the arc, heat sized for a closed joint drives deeper into the open root. Speed up a little or lower the amps across the gap.",
      "run_pq_heat": "On a long joint the plate keeps heating up. If you hold the same settings, what happens to penetration near the end?",
      "run_pq_heat_a": "It shrinks: a hot plate reflects the arc",
      "run_pq_heat_b": "It stays the same: settings fix penetration",
      "run_pq_heat_c": "It drops as the plate expands and thins",
      "run_pq_heat_d": "It creeps deeper: hot metal melts sooner",
      "run_pq_heat_wa": "A plate does not reflect much arc heat; a hotter plate is simply closer to melting.",
      "run_pq_heat_wb": "Settings fix the heat delivered, not how far it melts. The temperature of the base metal changes that.",
      "run_pq_heat_wc": "Thermal expansion here is thousandths of an inch; the bigger effect is the plate starting closer to its melting point.",
      "run_pq_heat_why": "Preheated metal needs less energy to reach melting, so the same arc melts deeper. Welders ease the travel speed up (or the amps down) as a long joint heats.",
      "run_pq_step": "Halfway along the joint the plate thins from 1/4 in. to 1/8 in. What should you do as the puddle reaches the thin plate?",
      "run_pq_step_a": "Speed up (or lower the amps): less heat per inch",
      "run_pq_step_b": "Slow down so the bead fills the thinner joint",
      "run_pq_step_c": "Raise the amps to keep the puddle fluid",
      "run_pq_step_d": "Nothing: the arc cannot sense plate thickness",
      "run_pq_step_wb": "Slowing down puts MORE heat into each inch, the opposite of what thin plate needs: it burns through.",
      "run_pq_step_wc": "More amps means more heat and deeper penetration: a fast route to burn-through on thin plate.",
      "run_pq_step_wd": "True, the arc cannot tell, and that is the problem: it keeps delivering heat sized for 1/4 in. plate.",
      "run_pq_step_why": "Thin plate needs less heat per inch. Heat input is V × A × 60 ÷ travel speed, so going faster (or turning the amps down) cuts it. Settings that were right on 1/4 in. can melt right through 1/8 in.",
