      "run_tl_key_in": "Short solid bar: in spec",
      "run_tl_key_out": "Tall striped bar: out of spec",
      "run_tn_ext": "Run the same tier three times and record the in-spec score, the travel speed you used on each plate thickness, and the plate temperature at the end. Plot travel speed against plate thickness and against plate temperature. Then open the Bead Lab, set the same volts and amps, and check whether your thin-plate travel speed lands its penetration near 0.6-0.75 of 1/8 in. Representative classroom numbers; a real procedure comes from the WPS for the joint.",
      "run_tn_m1": "\"Set the machine right and the weld takes care of itself.\" The joint changes under the torch (thickness, fit-up, heat build-up), so the welder's hands are part of the control loop.",
      "run_tn_m2": "\"Slower is always safer.\" Slowing down adds heat per inch: on thin plate or over a gap it causes burn-through.",
      "run_tn_m3": "\"Stickout does not matter.\" On a constant-voltage MIG machine a longer stickout lowers the current, so penetration sags even with the same settings.",
      "run_tn_m4": "\"A high score means a fast run.\" The score is the share of the bead in spec, not the time taken.",
      "run_tn_q1": "The plate thins from 1/4 in. to 1/8 in. halfway along. Using heat input = V × A × 60 ÷ travel speed, explain why speeding up is the fix, and estimate how much faster you must go to halve the heat per inch.",
      "run_tn_q2": "Two students both score 70%. One lost points at the thickness step, the other through heat build-up at the end. What different habits would you coach each of them on?",
      "run_tn_q3": "Why does a real welding procedure (WPS) give a RANGE for travel speed and amperage rather than one number? Connect your answer to the disturbances in this run.",
      "run_tn_q4": "A shipyard tier has the tightest band and the biggest disturbances. Why would a hull weld demand more consistency than a shop bracket?",
      "run_to_beadlab": "Try volts, amps and travel speed in the Bead Lab →",
