      "safe6_hoc_worker": "Worker",
      "safe7_caption": "1–3 identify parts of the secondary welding circuit. P identifies equipment-frame grounding and an installation boundary; details beyond that boundary are omitted. The dashed circle only locates the electrode/arc region.",
      "safe7_electrode": "Electrode lead",
      "safe7_electrode_text": "The electrode lead connects a welding output to the electrode holder or torch.",
      "safe7_frame": "Equipment frame grounding",
      "safe7_frame_text": "Equipment grounding concerns the machine frame or case. It has a different protective role from the work lead. The installation beyond the boundary is intentionally not specified here.",
      "safe7_hazard_detail_r2": "The work (return) lead is part of the welding circuit; equipment frame grounding has a different protective role. These terms are not interchangeable. The actual grounding arrangements depend on the equipment and installation requirements.",
      "safe7_hazard_title_r2": "Work lead and equipment grounding",
      "safe7_installation": "Equipment frame",
      "safe7_installation_legend": "P · Frame or case connection. The rest of the installation is not drawn. This reference does not specify a grounding arrangement.",
      "safe7_intro": "Choose a part to see its role. The simplified drawing separates the secondary welding circuit from equipment-frame grounding. Installation details are omitted.",
      "safe7_return": "Work (return) lead",
      "safe7_return_text": "The work lead connects the work terminal on the power source to the workpiece. It is a welding-current connection; calling it a ground lead can obscure the distinction from equipment grounding.",
      "safe7_roles": "Connection role to inspect",
      "safe7_scene_aria": "Conceptual welding connections and equipment frame. Highlighted: {value1}. Installation and electrical state are not specified.",
      "safe7_scope": "Geometry and colors distinguish reference parts; they do not specify conductor identification colors or a wiring method. No polarity, voltage, current, energized state or fault response is represented. This incomplete diagram does not establish a safe installation.",
      "safe7_secondary": "Welding circuit (secondary)",
      "safe7_secondary_legend": "1 · Electrode lead; 2 · workpiece; 3 · work (return) lead. The two output terminals are shown without polarity.",
      "safe7_source_lincoln": "Lincoln Electric · machine grounding and welding connections",
      "safe7_source_miller": "Miller · work lead and equipment grounding",
      "safe7_source_osha": "OSHA 1910.254 · work-lead and equipment requirements",
      "safe7_sources": "Definitions and equipment context",
      "safe7_title": "Different connections, different roles",
      "safe7_workpiece": "Workpiece",
      "safe7_workpiece_text": "The workpiece is the metal being welded. The electrode/arc region and the work connection are shown beside this metal.",
      "safe8_boundary400": "UV / visible boundary",
      "safe8_boundary700": "Visible / near-IR boundary",
      "safe8_difference": "Absolute difference",
      "safe8_equal": "Set B equal to A",
      "safe8_input_label": "{value1} · wavelength (nm)",
      "safe8_intro": "Move A and B through the illustrated 200–1400 nm window. Both probes use the same linear wavelength scale. At 400 nm and 700 nm, the reading identifies a band boundary.",
      "safe8_ir": "Near-IR portion",
      "safe8_plot_aria": "Wavelength comparison on a fixed 200-to-1400 nanometre window. A: {value1} nm, {value2}. B: {value3} nm, {value4}.",
      "safe8_radiation_intro_r2": "The illustrated 200–1400 nm window shows a UV portion (200–400 nm), visible light (400–700 nm), and a near-IR portion (700–1400 nm). All three damage eyes. Damage from UV is the most common — arc flash (photokeratitis) feels like sand in your eyes 6-12 hours later. Repeated exposure → cataracts + retinal damage.",
      "safe8_ratio": "Wavelength ratio B : A",
      "safe8_scope": "The display is a selected window of the spectrum. Colors distinguish the bands; the probes compare wavelength only.",
      "safe8_slider_label": "{value1} wavelength slider in nanometres",
      "safe8_source": "CCOHS · welding radiation bands",
      "safe8_swap": "Swap A and B",
      "safe8_title": "Compare two wavelengths",
      "safe8_uv": "UV portion",
      "safe8_visible": "Visible light",
      "safe9_axes": "Upper graph: sound level, 85–110 dBA, across; reference duration, 0–8 hours, upward. Both models use the same fixed linear axes. The two lower bars share a 0–8-hour scale.",
      "safe9_increment": "{value1} dBA + {value2} × {value3} dB = {value4} dBA",
      "safe9_intro": "Explore zero to four exchange steps from each model’s own 8-hour criterion. Each step adds 5 dB in the OSHA model or 3 dB in the NIOSH model, while halving the reference duration. This separate example leaves your entered tasks unchanged.",
      "safe9_legend": "O · OSHA: solid curve and circular marker. N · NIOSH: dashed curve and diamond marker. The horizontal guide links equal reference durations at different sound levels.",
      "safe9_niosh": "NIOSH · recommended exposure model",
      "safe9_osha": "OSHA · general-industry PEL model",
      "safe9_plot_aria": "Shared-scale exchange-rate graph, step {value1}. OSHA: {value2} dBA for {value3} hours. NIOSH: {value4} dBA for {value5} hours. Both lower duration bars have equal length. Complete equations follow.",
      "safe9_presets": "Exchange-step references",
      "safe9_scope": "Each duration shown reaches 100% dose for a constant sound level in its respective model. The OSHA reference uses the general-industry PEL criterion, 90 dBA for 8 hours; NIOSH recommends 85 dBA for 8 hours. These reference durations are not a guarantee against hearing damage or permission to work without controls. The existing calculator below combines your entered tasks.",
      "safe9_source_niosh": "NIOSH · 3-dB exchange rate",
      "safe9_source_osha": "OSHA · noise dose and reference duration",
      "safe9_step_aria": "{value1} exchange steps above each model’s criterion",
      "safe9_step_button": "Step {value1}",
      "safe9_step_label": "Exchange steps above each criterion: {value1}",
      "safe9_title": "Different decibel steps, the same time halving",
      "saltwater_conducts_equipment_must_be_d_r2": "Saltwater conducts. Wet welding is done on DC, normally electrode-negative (straight polarity), which also slows electrolytic corrosion of the electrode holder. Gear is fully insulated, and a topside knife switch keeps the circuit dead until the diver calls for it. AC is avoided because it is a greater shock hazard to the diver; follow AWS D3.6M and ADCI diving-safety practice.",
      "scene_options_hint": "Joint, welding position and inspection overlays",
      "shop3_detail_aria": "{value1}, enlarged station. Controls reflect recorded audit answers.",
      "shop3_electrical": "Leads and work return",
      "shop3_electrical_clue": "Inspect the welding leads, glove and floor conditions, and the work-clamp connection.",
      "shop3_ergo": "Work positioning",
      "shop3_ergo_clue": "Look for a positioner, suitable standing mat and planned posture breaks.",
      "shop3_eyes": "Arc protection",
      "shop3_eyes_clue": "Inspect the screen, helmet and safety-glasses provision.",
