      "uw6_hazard_visibility_r2": "Stirred-up sediment can cut visibility to inches. Surface-supplied divers need operational two-way voice communication with the surface team and continuous tending; a fixed check-in interval is not a substitute.",
      "uw6_hazard_voice": "Communication and tending line",
      "uw7_assumptions": "Simplified seawater model: absolute pressure = 1 + depth ÷ 33, in atmospheres. At the surface, pressure is 1 atm and the reference gas volume is 100%. Boyle’s law keeps pressure × volume constant for this fixed gas amount and temperature.",
      "uw7_axes": "Across: absolute pressure, 1–11 atm. Upward: remaining volume, 0–100% of the surface volume.",
      "uw7_caption": "The white ring marks the fixed surface reference. The filled dot and dashed guides follow your example. The curve stays fixed: doubling absolute pressure halves the gas volume under these assumptions.",
      "uw7_depth_aria": "Approximately {value1} feet in the local gas-law example",
      "uw7_depth_control": "Local example depth",
      "uw7_identity": "Relative balance: (P ÷ 1 atm) × (V ÷ surface volume) = 1. Here, {value1} × {value2} ≈ 1.",
      "uw7_intro": "This separate reference holds the amount of gas and its temperature fixed. Move the example depth, or enter a remaining volume percentage to work backward. Both controls describe the same point; the Pressure Lab settings above stay as they are.",
      "uw7_note": "This is an idealized, constant-temperature gas reference using the lab’s existing pressure and width relationships. An actual welding arc bubble exchanges gas and heat; this graph is not a model of its full behavior. Readouts are rounded; controls and geometry retain unrounded values. Exploring here does not change the Pressure Lab depth, technique, clock or answers, and does not set a diving or welding procedure.",
      "uw7_plot_aria": "Fixed pressure-volume graph. The curve covers the example’s zero-to-300-foot depth range. Current point: approximately {value1} atmospheres absolute pressure and {value2}% of the surface gas volume. The white ring is the surface reference: 1 atmosphere and 100%.",
      "uw7_presets": "Gas-law example depths",
      "uw7_read_depth": "Local example depth",
      "uw7_read_pressure": "Absolute pressure",
      "uw7_read_volume": "Volume compared with its surface value",
      "uw7_read_width": "Drawn width compared with its surface value",
      "uw7_source": "NOAA · absolute-pressure conversions and Boyle’s law (PDF page 4)",
      "uw7_title": "Solve the gas-volume relationship both ways",
      "uw7_use_lab": "Use lab depth ({value1} ft)",
      "uw7_volume_control": "Remaining volume (% of surface volume)",
      "uw7_volume_help": "This reference spans approximately {value1}% to 100%. Entering a percentage solves depth = 33 × (100 ÷ percentage − 1). Values outside this range settle at the nearest endpoint when you leave the field. Clearing it keeps the current point.",
      "uw7_width_cube": "The lab’s schematic width ratio is the cube root of the volume ratio: {value1}³ ≈ {value2}. A width percentage is not a volume percentage.",
      "weldable_without_preheat_in_most_condi_r2": "Weldable without preheat in most conditions on thinner sections. Typical of mild structural steel such as A36; A572 Grade 50 often sits right at the 0.40 line.",
      "when_you_weld_carbon_steel_the_haz_hea_r2": "When you weld carbon steel, the HAZ right next to the weld is heated past A3 and becomes fully austenite (about {value1} °F / {value2} °C for 0.20 % carbon steel); a little further out it only passes A1 ({value3} °F / {value4} °C) and partly transforms. When you stop welding, that region cools. If it cools FAST (thick section, cold base metal, no preheat) — martensite forms in the HAZ → brittle → crack. Higher carbon content = more martensite = more crack risk. ",
      "workbench_controls": "Welding controls",
      "workbench_setup": "Process, material & plate",
      "workbench_stage": "Weld stage",
      "working_at_depth_on_compressed_gas_the_r2": "Working at depth on compressed gas, the diver-welder must follow strict ascent / decompression schedules. \"Bends\" can cause permanent neurological damage or death. The dive team uses applicable tables and maintains a depth-time profile.",
